And you'll also find links in this episode's podcast notes

41
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:02,320
And in the remainder of this episode of the podcast.

42
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:08,320
I've got something to talk about in Scout Master ship in seven minutes or less-

43
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:11,239
And it's always more than seven minutes, by the way,

44
00:03:11,239 --> 00:03:13,000
I haven't managed to fix that yet.

45
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Anyway, onward and upward. let's get started, shall we?

46
00:03:38,320 --> 00:04:06,160
I'm going back to a comment that I got some time ago and I believe I may have even mentioned

47
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:07,160
on the podcast,

48
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:10,320
But anyways, someone got in touch with me to say this:

49
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:15,480
My ninth grade son is upset with the new Cyberchip requirement for Star Rank.

50
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:23,640
He said he has to watch, quote dumb videos, unquote and write an electronics contract.

51
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:28,120
He said this is not scouts, this is for school and parents to do.

52
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:30,080
I hate that they're putting this stuff in.

53
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:31,960
Do you have any thoughts about this?

54
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:33,360
Well, yeah, I do.

55
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:34,360
I do,

56
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:39,880
And I feel like I want to apologize before I make those comments because they sound negative.

57
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:42,280
The point is not to be negative.

58
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:47,160
There's a lot of positive in what I'm about to say, but it is a critique of the Cyberchip

59
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:52,040
and the little cards and contracts that have found their way into scouting and become

60
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:56,720
a substitute for what may be a better way of going about this, a little bit closer

61
00:04:56,720 --> 00:05:02,000
to what you know we started with over a century ago.

62
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:06,920
And as far as the whole Cyberchip thing goes, I mean I couldn't agree with your son more

63
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:15,720
Clearly. the issues that are raised in this are important, but the Cyberchip requirements-

64
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:24,200
they're just more frustrating school type work that I don't understand how this fits into scouting.

65
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:26,840
Young people see this sort of thing coming a mile away.

66
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:29,200
I know I did. when I was that age.

67
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:33,080
I knew that somebody was coming at me with a spoonful of medicine.

68
00:05:33,080 --> 00:05:37,479
They were going to try and put it in. you know they were going to try and sugar it up a little bit,

69
00:05:37,479 --> 00:05:41,280
but they had a big message for me that they wanted me to get

70
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:48,000
And I was instantly going to resist getting that message, especially when they tried to be cool about it.

71
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,599
You know when they, when they tried to speak to me as a young person-

72
00:05:52,599 --> 00:05:57,480
I mean young people- know when what they're being required to do in school

73
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:03,920
or in scouts is inherently kind of unfair or ridiculous and they're not quite sure why.

74
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:10,160
I think they feel as though they're being manipulated and will be grudgingly get it over with as soon as possible

75
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:15,600
And that's really not a very receptive attitude in a learning environment,

76
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:17,880
whether it is in school or in scouts.

77
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:27,240
The thing about scouts is that we are in the business of building character, not compliance,

78
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:29,480
And they're very different things.

79
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:31,320
Let me explain what I mean

80
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:37,320
Scouting's ideals are all in the scout oath and the scout law

81
00:06:37,320 --> 00:06:43,440
And if you look at the cyber chip materials- and I did- I found only one reference to the scout law

82
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:49,840
And this was in an older document and there were no references to the scout oath.

83
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:55,640
The character development- if that's what we're about, it- requires active, ongoing relationships.

84
00:06:55,640 --> 00:07:01,120
It can't be reduced to rules and contracts or in schoolwork or a list of requirements.

85
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:10,479
We build character actively by first trusting our scouts to be as committed to doing good and being good as we are,

86
00:07:10,479 --> 00:07:22,680
And we build character through honest reflection and discussion aimed at internalizing the ideals that we have in the scout oath and law.

87
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:27,480
These essential things really are just woven into the fabric of scouting.

88
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:33,680
Scouting is not about compelling young people to comply with rules and contracts.

89
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:37,520
It's about providing opportunities for young people to grow.

90
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:48,960
Implicit in the idea of putting rules and contracts in front of people is the flip side of that is compelling them with a fear of punishment or shame.

91
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:54,800
Character development is the result of examining our internal understanding of our principles and ideas,

92
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:59,680
our interaction with friends and mentors and counselors and coaches,

93
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:05,640
and trying to approach it in another way is just kind of ham-handed.

94
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:10,200
I have no choice about the cyber chip requirement being fulfilled.

95
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:13,600
It's a rule. it's sitting there, it has to be done.

96
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:18,040
But I am not going to require scouts to carry the cyber chip card.

97
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:24,120
I don't use any cards. I don't use the Totenship card, don't use the Fireman chip thing,

98
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:33,760
because I think it's kind of demeaning to tell a scout our trust is conditioned by the terms of a contract or card they're carrying in their pocket.

99
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:35,960
I just have never been able to get on board with it.

100
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:42,840
The clear message that we send when we employ these methods is to say: you cannot be trusted.

101
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:48,720
Therefore, your obedience must be coerced or compelled in some way.

102
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:55,720
I mean, I would rather tell scouts that our set point, the place that we begin

103
00:08:55,720 --> 00:09:01,840
and the thing that defines everything that we do, is to strive to live the scout oath and law,

104
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:04,080
And I trust them to do that

105
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:09,920
And it's something we'll be working on every time we're together with their fellow scouts.

106
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:16,479
I don't think that our work as scouters is controlling the conduct of young people.

107
00:09:16,479 --> 00:09:23,839
I think we have a much greater challenge: to form their character as defined by the scout oath and law.

108
00:09:23,839 --> 00:09:29,040
Now, are those things a code of conduct? I don't think so.

109
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:37,120
Is it an ideology? No, Is it a religious text of some kind? No, It's none of those things.

110
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:48,400
The scout oath and law are ideals that have really defined what you would think of as positive, contributing, individual character throughout human history.

111
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:52,480
I don't think that's overreaching. I think it's really true.

112
00:09:52,480 --> 00:10:01,200
You know the 12 points of the scout law that we have in the BSA, and the other way is that the scout law is enumerated in other parts of the world.

113
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:10,240
If you look at them all the common ideals expressed would have been familiar to a human being anytime that we've been called a human being.

114
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:17,760
And it's just an ingenious, simple way to build character is to keep going back to the scout oath and law and say:

115
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:29,680
well, let's take a look at what this has to say about how we ought to order our lives, how we ought to be, how we ought to treat other people, how we ought to treat ourselves

116
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:37,040
All over the world. the wording of Baden Powell's original oath and law has been adapted

117
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:41,840
And we've expanded our understanding of exactly what those ideals mean,

118
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:46,720
But the ideals themselves, I think, have remained fairly constant.

119
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:55,280
How do we tap into the vast character building potential of that oath and law?

120
00:10:55,280 --> 00:11:06,160
And I think we tap into it by understanding how rules differ from ideals and how conduct differs from character.

121
00:11:06,160 --> 00:11:14,000
Rules are different than ideals and conduct is different from character.

122
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:21,920
Rules control conduct. Ideals shape character.

123
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:26,719
Character is expressed in the way that you act in your conduct,

124
00:11:26,719 --> 00:11:34,079
But conduct does not necessarily define your character, especially when you're a young person.

125
00:11:34,079 --> 00:11:38,160
When you're a young person, you try lots of different ideas.

126
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:40,319
You try lots of different things.

127
00:11:40,319 --> 00:11:47,280
It doesn't mean that if you do a bad thing, that you are an inherently bad person.

128
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:53,040
it's not your conduct defining your character at all times, in all places, in every situation.

129
00:11:53,920 --> 00:12:01,920
Because when you're young you do untoward, silly things because you're growing up

130
00:12:01,920 --> 00:12:03,680
and you're learning about the world around you.

131
00:12:04,720 --> 00:12:11,120
So if you have a set of ideals to return to and say, gee, was my conduct consistent with

132
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:16,160
what my character ought to be according to these ideals, then there's a conversation starting.

133
00:12:16,719 --> 00:12:21,520
So the oath and law aren't rules that control conduct.

134
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:26,160
They're an expression of these lively ideals that shape character.

135
00:12:26,160 --> 00:12:30,160
And then we see that played out in the conduct of our scouts.

136
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:32,480
Thinking about rules, we have to remember something:

137
00:12:32,480 --> 00:12:36,400
Rules are imposed from the outside in.

138
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:40,560
The rules are something outside of you that somebody says: this is the rule

139
00:12:41,120 --> 00:12:46,720
and you're supposed to take it and internalize it and remember it and obey it.

140
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:53,120
We react to a list of rules much differently than we do. an expression of ideals,

141
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:58,720
Rules and contracts and cards and cyber chips and totem chips and things like that.

142
00:12:58,720 --> 00:13:04,000
they may be useful for defining generally acceptable behavior or routines,

143
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:09,600
but they are disappointingly power on this. when it comes to building character,

144
00:13:09,680 --> 00:13:10,560
Rules are weak.

145
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:20,160
They can be dissected and argued with and you can create excuses and lawyer your way around rules.

146
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:26,640
You can obey the letter of the law and just not really get the spirit of the law.

147
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:31,600
Rules are very weak when it comes to achieving the aims that we have as scouts.

148
00:13:31,600 --> 00:13:33,360
They're a very weak tool.

149
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:35,600
Now, should there be rules and policies and procedures?

150
00:13:35,680 --> 00:13:40,800
Well, yes, but the fewest and the simplest- to maintain safety and propriety,

151
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:45,920
and we should always be able to draw a line directly back to the scout oath and law

152
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:49,760
as the inspiration for having created those rules.

153
00:13:50,320 --> 00:13:54,160
We know that rules and ideals are different.

154
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:57,120
We know that conduct and character are different,

155
00:13:58,080 --> 00:14:01,840
And we know that rules are imposed from the outside in

156
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:06,560
and character is expressed from the inside out.

157
00:14:06,560 --> 00:14:10,000
Now you may be able to excuse your conduct.

158
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:15,760
I made a mistake or I wasn't thinking, or I had a lapse of character,

159
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:18,480
but you can't hide from who you are inside.

160
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:22,400
One of my- one of my pals, author, Kevin Callan,

161
00:14:23,120 --> 00:14:27,280
said something in the way that going camping is very revealing of character,

162
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:31,040
because you really can't pretend to be somebody else in the wilderness.

163
00:14:31,040 --> 00:14:33,760
You have to deal with the realities of life,

164
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:38,079
And I think that extends to being true to a lot of other situations too.

165
00:14:38,079 --> 00:14:40,160
You can't hide who you are.

166
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:43,760
You have to look in the mirror and you have to deal with that person.

167
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:47,360
And that's an important thing to understand when we're dealing with young people.

168
00:14:48,160 --> 00:14:51,040
A scout is to be trusted.

169
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:54,880
That's the original wording of the scout law

170
00:14:54,880 --> 00:14:59,040
And it's an aspiration and a declaration at the same time.

171
00:14:59,040 --> 00:15:01,199
A scout is to be trusted.

172
00:15:01,199 --> 00:15:03,920
And don't think I'm saying that you just kind of walk away and you say,

173
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:05,439
well, a scout's to be trusted,

174
00:15:05,439 --> 00:15:07,760
They'll do the right thing. and you walk away.

175
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:10,079
No, because you're smarter than that.

176
00:15:10,079 --> 00:15:11,520
You were a young person. once

177
00:15:11,520 --> 00:15:12,640
You know how to observe,

178
00:15:12,640 --> 00:15:15,599
You know what to look for, because they're going to try things.

179
00:15:15,599 --> 00:15:19,520
but it doesn't necessarily mean that they possess an inherently

180
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:20,959
untrustworthy nature.

181
00:15:20,959 --> 00:15:24,079
Once they understand the benefits of being trustworthy,

182
00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:30,400
they'll begin to adopt that aspect, that ideal, into their character.

183
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:32,400
It does actually work.

184
00:15:32,400 --> 00:15:34,160
What I'm trying to say is this:

185
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:39,680
Scouting is intended to develop an internal standard that drives outward conduct.

186
00:15:39,680 --> 00:15:42,800
We can't do that by just opposing rules from the outside.

187
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:48,480
We have to get on the inside and we have to have conversations and discussions.

188
00:15:49,040 --> 00:15:54,080
We have to take actions that develop that internal standard.

189
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:57,600
At first this seems difficult to do.

190
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:06,000
It may seem a little frightening and uncertain, and rules and regulations

191
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:12,480
and cards and chips might just seem, hey, that's just a much better way to go about it.

192
00:16:13,040 --> 00:16:17,760
Because now that the scout has heard all the rules and everything like that

193
00:16:17,760 --> 00:16:20,400
and is carrying around a card with a signature on it that says,

194
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:24,640
yes, that's my out, because now I can just pull that out and I can say,

195
00:16:24,640 --> 00:16:29,200
well, this is your fault, because obviously you understood these things at one time

196
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:31,280
and then you made a conscious decision not to do them.

197
00:16:32,480 --> 00:16:36,960
Yeah, I understand that, but you know how weak that is in building character.

198
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:42,480
Now you set yourself up into a confrontation over rules and things like that.

199
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:43,280
Come on,

200
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:45,280
It's not going to work for you

201
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:51,199
Working the other way, where, when you encounter difficult conduct or bad behavior.

202
00:16:51,199 --> 00:16:54,160
you sit down and you go through it and you talk about it and you say

203
00:16:54,160 --> 00:16:58,160
what part of the scout oath and law bears on this situation.

204
00:16:58,160 --> 00:16:59,439
What do you understand about it?

205
00:16:59,439 --> 00:17:03,680
How would you change the way that you handled that in the light of what we've discussed?

206
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:04,319
Okay,

207
00:17:04,319 --> 00:17:12,639
Those types of things. that approach is not certain, because life is full of pitfalls

208
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:18,080
and growing up is confounding and even a little frightening,

209
00:17:18,080 --> 00:17:26,000
And failures are not unusual or unexpected, and we ought to welcome the opportunity

210
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:35,360
for a scout to fail or to fall short of their own highest and best, because we can then turn

211
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:40,880
that into something positive that helps them develop an internal standard

212
00:17:40,960 --> 00:17:42,640
and helps them develop character.

213
00:17:43,200 --> 00:17:47,600
If we work to develop character, we not only negate bad behavior,

214
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:53,120
but we help our scouts form attitudes and ideals that will serve them throughout their lives.

215
00:17:53,120 --> 00:18:14,880
Well, thank you so much for listening to this 331st edition of the Scoutmaster podcast.

216
